(I may not be fully aware of the distinction you make between "na" and
"naku" for this particular case, but that doesn't mean I wouldn't
understand your explanation of it.)

The English expression "There is no ..." can lead to the following confusio=
n:

X that is broda (term) =3D zo'e noi ke'a broda =3D lo broda
no X that is broda (term) =3D no lo broda
There is no X that is broda. (proposition) =3D no lo broda cu zasti

all X (term) =3D ro da
All X are non-broda. (proposition) =3D ro da na'e broda

no lo broda cu zasti =3D(?) ro da na'e broda

The problem is the ambiguous predicate "there is (no)". Presumably you
meant "does not exit":

X that is broda does not exist. =3D lo broda na zasti

But that still doesn't seem to warrant its equation to "ro da na'e
broda", since we can refer to and talk about non-existing broda1 (e.g.
a flying teapot, a pink unicorn, Donald Duck, etc.) which must be
included in "ro da".


